(Discrepancy 1 (ENV): declaring a buffer DIRECTLY from an
 `environment's <property>` expression (`"NAME"`, `first`, or `last`
 all reproduce) never copies the string's bytes in. Content prints
 empty, `'s size` is -1 (never documented as a possible value --
 BUF-10 says size is "current bytes stored", a count, never negative),
 `'s capacity` is the usual dynamic-buffer initial reserve (4096, the
 same value BUF Discrepancy 1 already flags), and worst: `'s empty`
 reports FALSE despite the content being empty and the size being -1 --
 the property disagrees with both the content and the size of the very
 buffer it's a property of. Going through an intermediate `text`
 variable first (`a text called t is environment's "PATH". a buffer
 called b is t.`) works correctly (see ENV-03.vox) -- the bug is
 specific to a buffer's initializer being a `'s` property-read
 expression directly, not to `environment` data generally. D1b.vox
 shows the identical failure on `arguments's first`, so this is not
 environment-specific at the implementation level, even though it's
 this section's own leaf (`gen leaf environment inrange`,
 gen_misc.vox:82-90) that has been emitting the broken construct and
 asserting nothing about it (only ever reading `'s empty`, which is
 exactly the property that lies here).
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox D1.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   content:

   size:
   -1
   capacity:
   4096
   type:
   Buffer (static)
   empty:
   0
)
a buffer called pathbuf is environment's "PATH".
print "content:".
print pathbuf.
print "size:".
print pathbuf's size.
print "capacity:".
print pathbuf's capacity.
print "type:".
print pathbuf's type.
print "empty:".
print pathbuf's empty.
